WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.868
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:02.589 --> 00:00:04.380
ALLAN LIVINGSTON: Good
afternoon, everyone.

00:00:04.380 --> 00:00:05.910
Hi, how's it going?

00:00:05.910 --> 00:00:09.394
Thank you for coming
to our session.

00:00:09.394 --> 00:00:10.560
My name is Allan Livingston.

00:00:10.560 --> 00:00:13.530
I'm a product manager
on the G Suite team.

00:00:13.530 --> 00:00:16.980
And today, my
colleague, Chad, and I

00:00:16.980 --> 00:00:21.810
are going to run you through the
benefits of going cloud native

00:00:21.810 --> 00:00:25.110
and how that impacts TCO.

00:00:25.110 --> 00:00:30.870
So now, TCO is a
relatively heavy subject.

00:00:30.870 --> 00:00:37.740
So rather than diving right
in to looking at productivity

00:00:37.740 --> 00:00:39.360
suites, I'm going
to try to think

00:00:39.360 --> 00:00:41.880
about this in a more general
context and something

00:00:41.880 --> 00:00:46.340
that we can probably
all relate to.

00:00:46.340 --> 00:00:48.950
Something simple--
the light bulb.

00:00:48.950 --> 00:00:49.481
OK?

00:00:49.481 --> 00:00:50.980
So we all understand
the light bulb.

00:00:50.980 --> 00:00:55.170
And so we're looking at a
nice, let's say, 100 watt

00:00:55.170 --> 00:00:56.410
incandescent light bulb here.

00:00:59.080 --> 00:01:06.070
And what is the TCO of one of
these using sort of standard US

00:01:06.070 --> 00:01:06.640
power rates?

00:01:06.640 --> 00:01:07.140
Anybody?

00:01:07.140 --> 00:01:07.810
Any guesses?

00:01:07.810 --> 00:01:09.935
What the total cost of
ownership of that light bulb

00:01:09.935 --> 00:01:11.820
there would be including
the purchase price

00:01:11.820 --> 00:01:13.090
and the electricity costs?

00:01:13.090 --> 00:01:14.141
Any guesses at all?

00:01:14.141 --> 00:01:15.050
AUDIENCE: $50.

00:01:15.050 --> 00:01:16.133
ALLAN LIVINGSTON: Not bad.

00:01:16.133 --> 00:01:16.700
$32.

00:01:16.700 --> 00:01:18.520
$50 was the guess-- and $32.

00:01:18.520 --> 00:01:20.330
In some markets that
would be correct.

00:01:20.330 --> 00:01:23.590
So you got $32 there
from one of these guys.

00:01:23.590 --> 00:01:25.012
And that's kind of a lot.

00:01:25.012 --> 00:01:26.470
It's not great for
the environment,

00:01:26.470 --> 00:01:28.678
but it's also a lot of money
when you think about it.

00:01:28.678 --> 00:01:31.720
$32 for $1 or $2 light bulb.

00:01:31.720 --> 00:01:33.130
So these came along, right?

00:01:33.130 --> 00:01:35.050
Compact fluorescents.

00:01:35.050 --> 00:01:37.560
And so the idea behind
these is very simple.

00:01:37.560 --> 00:01:39.910
They're going to be a lot
more energy efficient,

00:01:39.910 --> 00:01:44.740
maybe six times more energy
efficient than a similar light.

00:01:44.740 --> 00:01:46.489
Any ideas what the
TCO on one of these

00:01:46.489 --> 00:01:48.280
is going to be for the
equivalent lifespan?

00:01:48.280 --> 00:01:51.950
They last longer, so you've
got to kind of prorate it down.

00:01:51.950 --> 00:01:53.090
Any ideas?

00:01:53.090 --> 00:01:55.740
Rough costs including
the purchase price?

00:01:55.740 --> 00:01:57.520
About $10 is about right.

00:01:57.520 --> 00:02:00.630
And so it depends on what
you get it for but about $10.

00:02:00.630 --> 00:02:05.260
And so significant
savings, right?

00:02:05.260 --> 00:02:10.660
And the payback period
on them is pretty quick.

00:02:10.660 --> 00:02:11.710
It's about six months.

00:02:11.710 --> 00:02:14.010
Depending on, again, you
know, the sort of a typical

00:02:14.010 --> 00:02:16.030
set up that you're going to see.

00:02:16.030 --> 00:02:18.582
So this is a project
that you would-- you

00:02:18.582 --> 00:02:20.790
know, if you're thinking
about investing in a project

00:02:20.790 --> 00:02:21.970
and you're thinking
rationally, it's

00:02:21.970 --> 00:02:23.345
probably a pretty
good investment

00:02:23.345 --> 00:02:24.592
to make-- these light bulbs.

00:02:24.592 --> 00:02:26.050
But then you're
going to say, well,

00:02:26.050 --> 00:02:27.880
there's the time
value of the money.

00:02:27.880 --> 00:02:29.050
It's the payoff.

00:02:29.050 --> 00:02:31.630
And again, I used
some variables here,

00:02:31.630 --> 00:02:35.920
but unless you're financing
it at 87% or higher rate,

00:02:35.920 --> 00:02:37.880
then it makes sense to get it.

00:02:37.880 --> 00:02:39.546
It actually makes
sense to just buy them

00:02:39.546 --> 00:02:41.440
on your credit card and
then not even pay the bill.

00:02:41.440 --> 00:02:43.210
That's what a good
investment they are.

00:02:43.210 --> 00:02:45.001
But there's one thing
that's kind of weird.

00:02:45.001 --> 00:02:47.470
And you see this-- is these
things never really caught on.

00:02:47.470 --> 00:02:50.050
And they've been around for--

00:02:50.050 --> 00:02:52.330
well, I've got 25 years
here, but they've actually

00:02:52.330 --> 00:02:55.060
been around longer in
other form factors.

00:02:55.060 --> 00:02:58.510
And you see them in
2000 kind of coming up,

00:02:58.510 --> 00:03:01.011
and you've actually seen them
kind of back down a little bit

00:03:01.011 --> 00:03:01.510
here.

00:03:01.510 --> 00:03:02.710
This is US adoption data.

00:03:02.710 --> 00:03:05.590
It's actually blended, I think,
Connecticut and California.

00:03:05.590 --> 00:03:09.250
But you see them coming
down, and then you

00:03:09.250 --> 00:03:11.110
see these kind of weird
bumps along the way.

00:03:11.110 --> 00:03:12.661
And what was happening
the whole time

00:03:12.661 --> 00:03:14.410
is there were a lot
of government programs

00:03:14.410 --> 00:03:17.500
and incentives to
just incentivize

00:03:17.500 --> 00:03:19.202
people to get these things.

00:03:19.202 --> 00:03:21.160
And even though the TCO
on them and the payback

00:03:21.160 --> 00:03:22.577
was already
ridiculously high, you

00:03:22.577 --> 00:03:24.660
know there were all these
incentives where you get

00:03:24.660 --> 00:03:26.316
them cheap or things like that.

00:03:26.316 --> 00:03:28.690
But then the retailers would
find people would come back,

00:03:28.690 --> 00:03:29.565
they'd get one cheap.

00:03:29.565 --> 00:03:30.640
And then they'd go--

00:03:30.640 --> 00:03:33.100
you know, they wouldn't
like it, or they'd come back

00:03:33.100 --> 00:03:35.141
and the program wasn't
there, so they'd recommend

00:03:35.141 --> 00:03:36.474
putting incandescent back in.

00:03:36.474 --> 00:03:38.140
And there was another
problem with them,

00:03:38.140 --> 00:03:41.740
which was they're actually
a deficient product.

00:03:41.740 --> 00:03:42.520
Right?

00:03:42.520 --> 00:03:45.550
And they're deficient for
a whole bunch of reasons.

00:03:45.550 --> 00:03:48.910
Most people find the light
quality in them not very great.

00:03:48.910 --> 00:03:50.380
They flicker a bit.

00:03:50.380 --> 00:03:52.390
The form factor, they
tend to stick out.

00:03:52.390 --> 00:03:54.265
They're getting better
now, but they actually

00:03:54.265 --> 00:03:55.060
tend to stick out.

00:03:55.060 --> 00:03:57.192
A lot of the older
ones had slow start up,

00:03:57.192 --> 00:03:59.150
and they're not compatible
with dimmer switches

00:03:59.150 --> 00:04:00.670
and some other applications.

00:04:00.670 --> 00:04:05.050
So you look at that, you've got
a product with compelling TCO

00:04:05.050 --> 00:04:05.920
arguments.

00:04:05.920 --> 00:04:10.420
Really far and away a slam
dunk as far as investment

00:04:10.420 --> 00:04:14.640
and yet it struggles
to catch on.

00:04:14.640 --> 00:04:17.350
So TCO by itself
isn't enough, which

00:04:17.350 --> 00:04:19.089
brings us to this guy here.

00:04:19.089 --> 00:04:21.130
LED light bulb.

00:04:21.130 --> 00:04:22.870
So I ran the same
kind of numbers

00:04:22.870 --> 00:04:24.470
on these, same power rates.

00:04:24.470 --> 00:04:26.020
TCO is a bit better on this--

00:04:26.020 --> 00:04:26.891
about $7.

00:04:26.891 --> 00:04:29.140
They're a little more
efficient, and they last longer.

00:04:29.140 --> 00:04:30.848
So you can amortize
the cost over longer,

00:04:30.848 --> 00:04:32.200
and so it brings the TCO down.

00:04:32.200 --> 00:04:33.700
They were already compelling--

00:04:33.700 --> 00:04:35.040
compact fluorescents
were already compelling.

00:04:35.040 --> 00:04:36.206
This is a little bit better.

00:04:38.660 --> 00:04:41.680
And you're financing, again,
as long as your credit card--

00:04:41.680 --> 00:04:45.040
which I hope it isn't-- is
charging you less than 112%

00:04:45.040 --> 00:04:47.589
a month, you're good to just
charge it on your credit card

00:04:47.589 --> 00:04:48.880
and not even pay the bill back.

00:04:48.880 --> 00:04:50.046
It's such a good investment.

00:04:50.046 --> 00:04:51.610
So solid, solid investment.

00:04:51.610 --> 00:04:55.242
So it's got the same kind of
financial characteristics,

00:04:55.242 --> 00:04:56.950
but it's actually
seeing better adoption.

00:04:56.950 --> 00:04:58.750
So this is the
same data overlaid,

00:04:58.750 --> 00:05:01.360
where you've got the blue
is the compact fluorescent,

00:05:01.360 --> 00:05:03.550
and then the red is
the LED light bulbs.

00:05:03.550 --> 00:05:08.020
And you're seeing a very sharp
uptick in the LED adoption

00:05:08.020 --> 00:05:11.890
and a corresponding decrease
in the compact fluorescent.

00:05:11.890 --> 00:05:16.000
And it's not explained by that
relatively modest difference

00:05:16.000 --> 00:05:17.380
in TCO.

00:05:17.380 --> 00:05:20.170
It's clearly explained by
the product characteristics

00:05:20.170 --> 00:05:21.170
themselves.

00:05:21.170 --> 00:05:23.020
It's better light quality.

00:05:23.020 --> 00:05:23.990
Form factor is great.

00:05:23.990 --> 00:05:26.140
You can identically
match incandescent.

00:05:26.140 --> 00:05:28.470
You can also have other form
factors, startup's good.

00:05:28.470 --> 00:05:30.470
And they're compatible
with dimmer applications.

00:05:30.470 --> 00:05:31.950
The dimmer switch is
another application.

00:05:31.950 --> 00:05:33.730
So it's just a superior
product, right?

00:05:33.730 --> 00:05:37.420
It does exactly what the
incandescent light bulb does,

00:05:37.420 --> 00:05:38.660
but it's got a better TCO.

00:05:38.660 --> 00:05:40.420
It's not an inferior product.

00:05:40.420 --> 00:05:41.920
It also does other things.

00:05:41.920 --> 00:05:44.506
Because they're so small,
you've got new formats.

00:05:44.506 --> 00:05:45.880
And the extreme
long life of them

00:05:45.880 --> 00:05:48.322
allows you to permanently
put them into some fixtures,

00:05:48.322 --> 00:05:50.530
and you don't even have to
have them to be removable.

00:05:50.530 --> 00:05:55.480
So it looks like a product with
a really strong runway because

00:05:55.480 --> 00:05:56.690
of this.

00:05:56.690 --> 00:05:58.360
So let's talk about another one.

00:05:58.360 --> 00:06:00.090
Well, let's kind
of lay this out.

00:06:00.090 --> 00:06:03.740
And so we've got our TCO
getting better as you go up

00:06:03.740 --> 00:06:06.500
and the product benefits
getting better as you right.

00:06:06.500 --> 00:06:07.735
Nice, simple two by two.

00:06:07.735 --> 00:06:09.110
Really, what it
looks like is you

00:06:09.110 --> 00:06:12.740
need both in this
world to be successful.

00:06:12.740 --> 00:06:15.170
You need to have both a
compelling product that's

00:06:15.170 --> 00:06:18.260
really usable and really
good for the purpose and TCO.

00:06:18.260 --> 00:06:20.270
Because people aren't
just number crunchers.

00:06:20.270 --> 00:06:22.350
They need something
that makes sense.

00:06:22.350 --> 00:06:25.760
Let's talk about
another common case.

00:06:25.760 --> 00:06:29.480
We've got ourselves
a hybrid car.

00:06:29.480 --> 00:06:30.775
And so what do hybrid cars do?

00:06:30.775 --> 00:06:32.150
Well, typically
they've got a bit

00:06:32.150 --> 00:06:35.570
of a premium over the
conventional cars,

00:06:35.570 --> 00:06:39.410
and they've got a bit of
a better fuel economy.

00:06:39.410 --> 00:06:42.270
But with the way gas
prices are right now,

00:06:42.270 --> 00:06:44.590
it's actually not really
a compelling TCO buy.

00:06:44.590 --> 00:06:46.820
And so this is a
study that was done

00:06:46.820 --> 00:06:50.450
on 2016 models, a projection
with current gas prices.

00:06:50.450 --> 00:06:54.260
And it found that only
24% of hybrid cars

00:06:54.260 --> 00:06:58.642
had a projected TCO lower than
the equivalent conventionally

00:06:58.642 --> 00:07:00.350
fueled cars, because
the premium was just

00:07:00.350 --> 00:07:02.960
too high for the savings.

00:07:02.960 --> 00:07:05.482
And all things being equal,
some people prefer hybrids,

00:07:05.482 --> 00:07:07.190
some people don't like
the way they drive

00:07:07.190 --> 00:07:08.330
and things like that.

00:07:08.330 --> 00:07:10.010
Let's even just call them equal.

00:07:10.010 --> 00:07:17.420
That itself is not a great
trend for hybrid cars.

00:07:17.420 --> 00:07:21.860
And you see that in the relative
penetration of US auto sales.

00:07:21.860 --> 00:07:23.630
And so they actually
are coming down

00:07:23.630 --> 00:07:24.850
as the fuel prices come down.

00:07:24.850 --> 00:07:27.800
So even the price is probably
equal, or some people

00:07:27.800 --> 00:07:28.970
for one way or the other.

00:07:28.970 --> 00:07:31.142
But without the TCO
element there, it

00:07:31.142 --> 00:07:32.600
doesn't make a
whole bunch of sense

00:07:32.600 --> 00:07:37.284
to ask people to put that
investment in ahead of time

00:07:37.284 --> 00:07:38.700
and then not even
see the payback.

00:07:38.700 --> 00:07:40.366
There's certainly
environmental benefits

00:07:40.366 --> 00:07:43.504
which appeals to a
niche of the population,

00:07:43.504 --> 00:07:45.920
but the straight dollars and
cents just don't bear it out.

00:07:45.920 --> 00:07:49.640
And so it would seemed
like if hybrid cars stayed,

00:07:49.640 --> 00:07:51.770
don't get their
characteristics fixed up,

00:07:51.770 --> 00:07:56.040
that they don't
have a great set up.

00:07:56.040 --> 00:07:58.800
So with that out of the way--

00:07:58.800 --> 00:07:59.940
just kind of some framing.

00:07:59.940 --> 00:08:03.964
Let's apply this to a lot
more complicated subject which

00:08:03.964 --> 00:08:05.130
you guys actually came here.

00:08:05.130 --> 00:08:06.270
Because I'm guessing
you probably

00:08:06.270 --> 00:08:08.370
didn't come down to the
basement of the Marriott

00:08:08.370 --> 00:08:11.160
on a lovely afternoon to
talk about light bulbs.

00:08:11.160 --> 00:08:13.620
So we're going to talk
about Sass applications

00:08:13.620 --> 00:08:15.810
and productivity software.

00:08:15.810 --> 00:08:19.320
And so just like
light bulbs, we've

00:08:19.320 --> 00:08:22.150
got to consider more
than just the cost

00:08:22.150 --> 00:08:23.350
when we're looking at this.

00:08:23.350 --> 00:08:25.980
You've also got to look at
the product benefits, the fit,

00:08:25.980 --> 00:08:27.939
and also what kind
of new possibilities

00:08:27.939 --> 00:08:30.480
it opens up, the product, just
like we saw with the LED light

00:08:30.480 --> 00:08:31.440
bulbs.

00:08:31.440 --> 00:08:34.020
And so when we're thinking
about the G Suite,

00:08:34.020 --> 00:08:37.049
we're thinking about
three primary areas here.

00:08:37.049 --> 00:08:39.480
There's the productivity
enhancements.

00:08:39.480 --> 00:08:42.000
There's cost reductions.

00:08:42.000 --> 00:08:44.820
And then there's
replacement savings

00:08:44.820 --> 00:08:46.600
that you're going to get.

00:08:46.600 --> 00:08:50.280
And so with that I'm going
to ask my colleague Chad

00:08:50.280 --> 00:08:52.770
to come up and run us
in a little more detail

00:08:52.770 --> 00:08:56.280
through the way that we
see the G Suite delivering

00:08:56.280 --> 00:08:59.050
productivity benefits that
really help the bottom line.

00:09:01.985 --> 00:09:02.610
CHAD TYLER: OK.

00:09:02.610 --> 00:09:03.300
Thanks, Allan.

00:09:03.300 --> 00:09:06.360
We wanted to start off by
talking about productivity

00:09:06.360 --> 00:09:08.400
because these
benefits aren't always

00:09:08.400 --> 00:09:10.590
as obvious at first
glance, but they're

00:09:10.590 --> 00:09:13.880
a key part of the value
that G Suite offers.

00:09:13.880 --> 00:09:16.260
We want to think
about things like what

00:09:16.260 --> 00:09:19.530
is possible in G Suite
and the cloud that

00:09:19.530 --> 00:09:24.300
wasn't possible in an on-premise
or even hybrid environment.

00:09:24.300 --> 00:09:26.250
How can we take
collaboration and make it

00:09:26.250 --> 00:09:29.220
a really powerful tool
that can make people more

00:09:29.220 --> 00:09:31.560
efficient and more effective?

00:09:31.560 --> 00:09:33.480
And how can we
engage and connect

00:09:33.480 --> 00:09:36.360
with even a remote
and deskless workers

00:09:36.360 --> 00:09:39.570
so that we can bring them
in and make them more--

00:09:39.570 --> 00:09:40.962
closer part of the team.

00:09:40.962 --> 00:09:42.420
And so these are
some of the things

00:09:42.420 --> 00:09:47.710
we want to think about as we go
through this next section here.

00:09:47.710 --> 00:09:49.950
The first thing that
I want to touch on

00:09:49.950 --> 00:09:54.030
is real time collaboration.

00:09:54.030 --> 00:09:56.190
Forrester recently
did a study that

00:09:56.190 --> 00:09:59.160
showed that when
multiple editors can work

00:09:59.160 --> 00:10:03.090
on the same document, slide,
or sheet at the same time,

00:10:03.090 --> 00:10:06.060
it actually reduces the
amount of time it takes

00:10:06.060 --> 00:10:09.480
to create that content by 20%.

00:10:09.480 --> 00:10:11.670
This translates into
about 15 minutes

00:10:11.670 --> 00:10:14.479
to two hours per
employee, per week,

00:10:14.479 --> 00:10:16.020
depending on the
type of role they're

00:10:16.020 --> 00:10:19.770
in or how collaborative
that employee tends to be.

00:10:19.770 --> 00:10:21.930
But beyond just
the time savings,

00:10:21.930 --> 00:10:26.580
they also found that this type
of collaboration makes people--

00:10:26.580 --> 00:10:29.280
makes meetings more
effective as well.

00:10:29.280 --> 00:10:31.950
Because since people have been
working on the same document

00:10:31.950 --> 00:10:35.402
together, they've seen how
the content has evolved,

00:10:35.402 --> 00:10:37.110
and how they've gotten
to where they are.

00:10:37.110 --> 00:10:39.440
They understand
the history of it.

00:10:39.440 --> 00:10:42.210
And they're also all looking
at the same version of it.

00:10:42.210 --> 00:10:43.920
So when they get
to a meeting, they

00:10:43.920 --> 00:10:47.850
spend a lot less time working
to try to get on the same page

00:10:47.850 --> 00:10:49.590
and trying to make
sure everybody's

00:10:49.590 --> 00:10:52.230
looking at the same thing,
and they can dive right

00:10:52.230 --> 00:10:55.050
into the core part
of that meeting

00:10:55.050 --> 00:10:57.360
and the core purpose
of the meeting.

00:10:57.360 --> 00:11:00.750
So both of those
have a huge impact

00:11:00.750 --> 00:11:03.360
on how effective
employees can be.

00:11:03.360 --> 00:11:05.550
I also want to hit
on Hangouts as well.

00:11:05.550 --> 00:11:08.820
Because what Hangouts does
is it creates an environment

00:11:08.820 --> 00:11:13.560
where it's very easy to
engage with other teammates

00:11:13.560 --> 00:11:14.209
over video.

00:11:14.209 --> 00:11:15.750
Where you can have
those face-to-face

00:11:15.750 --> 00:11:18.210
chats really quickly
instead of having

00:11:18.210 --> 00:11:20.790
to rely on slower
forms of communication,

00:11:20.790 --> 00:11:22.440
like email, where
there tends to be

00:11:22.440 --> 00:11:25.260
some latency and some
lag between asking

00:11:25.260 --> 00:11:27.210
questions and getting answers.

00:11:27.210 --> 00:11:31.440
So Hangouts is also a
key part of the real time

00:11:31.440 --> 00:11:34.450
collaboration within G Suite.

00:11:34.450 --> 00:11:39.000
Mobility is also something
I want to touch on.

00:11:39.000 --> 00:11:42.820
In today's world with the
prevalence of mobile devices,

00:11:42.820 --> 00:11:46.930
it's more important than ever
that employees and people

00:11:46.930 --> 00:11:48.970
within an organization
can access

00:11:48.970 --> 00:11:53.590
the information they need from
any device and from anywhere.

00:11:53.590 --> 00:11:55.600
And G Suite enables this.

00:11:55.600 --> 00:12:00.220
Whether you're on Android,
or iOS, or a laptop,

00:12:00.220 --> 00:12:04.330
using Google Drive your
files are there at any time,

00:12:04.330 --> 00:12:08.510
and they're easy to access,
they're easy to work with.

00:12:08.510 --> 00:12:13.330
So mobility is something
else that's really key.

00:12:13.330 --> 00:12:17.660
Beyond just being able to
access the information though,

00:12:17.660 --> 00:12:21.290
G Suite also enables people to
use their own personal devices.

00:12:21.290 --> 00:12:23.860
So instead of having to
adapt to a new device

00:12:23.860 --> 00:12:27.160
and the differences between
that and what they normally use,

00:12:27.160 --> 00:12:29.830
they can use something
that's comfortable to them

00:12:29.830 --> 00:12:32.710
and be more focused on what
they're trying to do and less

00:12:32.710 --> 00:12:35.620
on how they're trying to do it.

00:12:35.620 --> 00:12:38.440
I want to hit on
Hangouts again here,

00:12:38.440 --> 00:12:43.240
specifically how management
communicates with their teams.

00:12:43.240 --> 00:12:47.590
Forrester also found that
when management effectively

00:12:47.590 --> 00:12:50.920
uses Hangouts, they can reduce
the amount of travel time

00:12:50.920 --> 00:12:51.970
they have to do by 40%.

00:12:54.520 --> 00:12:58.810
So beyond just having
a reduction in the time

00:12:58.810 --> 00:13:01.870
they have to spend traveling
and the budget that goes along

00:13:01.870 --> 00:13:04.690
with that, that let's
management focus

00:13:04.690 --> 00:13:07.030
a lot more on their
important tasks

00:13:07.030 --> 00:13:09.460
than jumping on an airplane
to try to fly somewhere

00:13:09.460 --> 00:13:12.870
to communicate with their team.

00:13:12.870 --> 00:13:16.590
This is just a quote by Keith
Chisholm, who at the time

00:13:16.590 --> 00:13:19.200
was a program director
at Rentokil Initial.

00:13:19.200 --> 00:13:23.430
And they actually saw this
benefit within their company.

00:13:23.430 --> 00:13:26.930
They saw that when they
started using Hangouts

00:13:26.930 --> 00:13:28.512
in the leadership
team, they were

00:13:28.512 --> 00:13:29.970
able to communicate
with their team

00:13:29.970 --> 00:13:31.602
and really connect
with them and have

00:13:31.602 --> 00:13:33.060
a really good
understanding of what

00:13:33.060 --> 00:13:34.650
was going on with their team.

00:13:34.650 --> 00:13:37.200
And at the same time they were
able to reduce their travel

00:13:37.200 --> 00:13:40.260
by 40%.

00:13:40.260 --> 00:13:44.700
This, again, beyond just
the big budget decrease

00:13:44.700 --> 00:13:46.920
that that had on
their company, it also

00:13:46.920 --> 00:13:49.080
increased the communication
and the impact

00:13:49.080 --> 00:13:51.270
that leadership could
have while still

00:13:51.270 --> 00:13:54.150
giving them more time
to focus on other tasks

00:13:54.150 --> 00:13:57.410
that they needed to get to.

00:13:57.410 --> 00:14:01.580
Now, when I talk about
social engagement,

00:14:01.580 --> 00:14:04.790
your first thought might
not be productivity.

00:14:04.790 --> 00:14:10.130
But what social, and in the case
of G Suite, what Google+ does,

00:14:10.130 --> 00:14:14.690
is it enables people
to form connections.

00:14:14.690 --> 00:14:16.640
Beyond the formality
of a meeting

00:14:16.640 --> 00:14:19.460
or the brevity of a
hallway conversation,

00:14:19.460 --> 00:14:22.670
it lets people
share ideas, and it

00:14:22.670 --> 00:14:26.540
lets them share their interests
with each other, regardless

00:14:26.540 --> 00:14:29.750
of their location,
regardless of their role.

00:14:29.750 --> 00:14:33.560
So as they start to form
these more social connections

00:14:33.560 --> 00:14:35.590
with the people that
are around them,

00:14:35.590 --> 00:14:37.910
they become more
and more engaged

00:14:37.910 --> 00:14:41.150
in what they're working on
and who they are working with.

00:14:41.150 --> 00:14:46.400
PwC did a study that showed
when employees are engaged,

00:14:46.400 --> 00:14:50.480
they actually are 30% more
satisfied with their jobs,

00:14:50.480 --> 00:14:53.970
and they generate
32% more ideas.

00:14:53.970 --> 00:14:57.680
So what social
engagement does is

00:14:57.680 --> 00:15:01.370
it makes people more
invested in their workplace.

00:15:01.370 --> 00:15:05.120
It makes people more excited
about what they're working on.

00:15:05.120 --> 00:15:08.120
It increases the
retention of people

00:15:08.120 --> 00:15:10.010
staying within their
workplace and staying

00:15:10.010 --> 00:15:12.080
within their
organization and helps

00:15:12.080 --> 00:15:14.826
them to have a bigger impact
because they're more invested

00:15:14.826 --> 00:15:15.950
in what they're working on.

00:15:15.950 --> 00:15:18.500
They're more invested in the
goals of that organization

00:15:18.500 --> 00:15:20.340
and what they're
trying to accomplish.

00:15:20.340 --> 00:15:24.440
So social within an
enterprise can have

00:15:24.440 --> 00:15:28.130
a huge impact, a
profound change in how

00:15:28.130 --> 00:15:32.704
employees feel about where they
are and what they're doing.

00:15:32.704 --> 00:15:35.120
And then the last thing I want
to touch on in this section

00:15:35.120 --> 00:15:37.790
is extensibility.

00:15:37.790 --> 00:15:40.790
Through the APIs
that are offered

00:15:40.790 --> 00:15:44.120
through G Suite and a
tool called App Maker,

00:15:44.120 --> 00:15:49.080
organizations can
integrate their systems

00:15:49.080 --> 00:15:51.860
and infrastructure to
be part of the suite.

00:15:51.860 --> 00:15:55.340
So basically that means
that all of your information

00:15:55.340 --> 00:15:57.590
and all of your data
can work together

00:15:57.590 --> 00:16:00.470
to create solutions that weren't
possible before when everything

00:16:00.470 --> 00:16:03.854
was segmented and segregated.

00:16:03.854 --> 00:16:06.020
I want to touch a little
bit more on App Maker, too.

00:16:06.020 --> 00:16:09.170
Because what App
Maker is is it's

00:16:09.170 --> 00:16:11.570
a tool that enables
organizations

00:16:11.570 --> 00:16:15.800
to very easily create
powerful tools that

00:16:15.800 --> 00:16:19.370
can automate processes that
employees and people would

00:16:19.370 --> 00:16:21.860
normally do over and over again.

00:16:21.860 --> 00:16:24.980
So App Maker takes
those redundant tasks

00:16:24.980 --> 00:16:27.650
that people are working
on and automates them

00:16:27.650 --> 00:16:31.190
so that they can focus on
things that have more impact

00:16:31.190 --> 00:16:35.010
and are more central to the
goals of the organization.

00:16:35.010 --> 00:16:38.410
App Maker's something that
we're really excited about.

00:16:38.410 --> 00:16:40.010
And I encourage everyone--

00:16:40.010 --> 00:16:43.980
during the product
roadmap session on Friday,

00:16:43.980 --> 00:16:47.740
they're going to be doing some
demonstrations of App Maker,

00:16:47.740 --> 00:16:50.240
and how you can use it, and how
you can build tools with it.

00:16:50.240 --> 00:16:53.710
I'd encourage everyone, if you
have a chance to attend that,

00:16:53.710 --> 00:16:56.360
because it's something
that we believe can really

00:16:56.360 --> 00:16:59.430
change how people work.

00:16:59.430 --> 00:17:02.280
Now, I've talked about a
lot of classic examples

00:17:02.280 --> 00:17:03.210
of productivity.

00:17:03.210 --> 00:17:06.960
I've talked about how we can
take what people are doing

00:17:06.960 --> 00:17:08.510
and make them more
efficient at it,

00:17:08.510 --> 00:17:12.520
how we can kind of change
how they approach problems.

00:17:12.520 --> 00:17:14.730
What I want to do now is
talk about how we're kind

00:17:14.730 --> 00:17:16.750
of going beyond productivity.

00:17:16.750 --> 00:17:19.650
How we're you using
Google Smarts and Machine

00:17:19.650 --> 00:17:24.000
Learning to start to
remove some of the tasks

00:17:24.000 --> 00:17:26.760
that people normally
would have to focus on.

00:17:26.760 --> 00:17:29.170
And honestly, this,
in my opinion,

00:17:29.170 --> 00:17:31.050
is where things get
really exciting.

00:17:31.050 --> 00:17:32.790
Because we are just
barely starting to

00:17:32.790 --> 00:17:34.380
scratch the surface
in this area.

00:17:34.380 --> 00:17:38.850
We're barely starting to
look and see what's possible.

00:17:38.850 --> 00:17:41.730
But as you'll see, even
these early examples

00:17:41.730 --> 00:17:43.920
can really change
how people work

00:17:43.920 --> 00:17:47.320
and can make them more
productive and more efficient.

00:17:47.320 --> 00:17:49.540
So we're excited about
what we can do here

00:17:49.540 --> 00:17:52.390
and what we can
bring to the market.

00:17:52.390 --> 00:17:56.430
But again, these
are just the start.

00:17:56.430 --> 00:17:58.380
So the first thing
I want to touch on

00:17:58.380 --> 00:18:01.080
is what's called Sheets Explore.

00:18:01.080 --> 00:18:03.510
What Sheets Explore
does is it enables

00:18:03.510 --> 00:18:08.820
people to use real world
language to build a query.

00:18:08.820 --> 00:18:11.700
So instead of having
to go into a sheet

00:18:11.700 --> 00:18:14.400
and figure out what
formulas they want to use,

00:18:14.400 --> 00:18:18.090
and how they put those formulas
together to answer a question,

00:18:18.090 --> 00:18:19.920
they can simply
type the question

00:18:19.920 --> 00:18:26.080
that they have, and sheets will
build the formula for them.

00:18:26.080 --> 00:18:29.340
Now, this is hugely important.

00:18:29.340 --> 00:18:33.240
Because what we have found
is that only about 30%

00:18:33.240 --> 00:18:36.780
of the people in an organization
know how to effectively build

00:18:36.780 --> 00:18:39.690
a formula and how to answer
a question based on data

00:18:39.690 --> 00:18:41.220
that they have.

00:18:41.220 --> 00:18:43.020
So if you think about
that, that means

00:18:43.020 --> 00:18:47.160
that 70% of the people
in your organization

00:18:47.160 --> 00:18:49.890
are answering questions
and are making decisions

00:18:49.890 --> 00:18:53.780
with less information than
they could and should have.

00:18:53.780 --> 00:18:55.230
That's huge.

00:18:55.230 --> 00:18:58.980
So Sheets Explore
opens up all the data

00:18:58.980 --> 00:19:01.200
that an organization
has to better

00:19:01.200 --> 00:19:06.150
inform and to better answer
questions that people have.

00:19:06.150 --> 00:19:09.030
We think this will have
a profound impact when

00:19:09.030 --> 00:19:12.480
widely used on how
people look at data.

00:19:12.480 --> 00:19:15.480
And you think about how
much data we have nowadays,

00:19:15.480 --> 00:19:17.730
and how that's just going
to be more and more as we

00:19:17.730 --> 00:19:19.350
move forward.

00:19:19.350 --> 00:19:21.450
We're really excited
about the impact

00:19:21.450 --> 00:19:24.570
that Sheets Explore can have.

00:19:24.570 --> 00:19:26.970
The next one is
called Slides Explore,

00:19:26.970 --> 00:19:29.400
or sometimes called Auto Layout.

00:19:29.400 --> 00:19:33.960
And what this does is it allows
people to put images or text

00:19:33.960 --> 00:19:37.405
on a slide, and then
use this feature to help

00:19:37.405 --> 00:19:39.780
them organize the slide and
help them figure out the what

00:19:39.780 --> 00:19:43.140
the layout should look like
and build a deck around it.

00:19:43.140 --> 00:19:48.510
What we've seen in measuring
this is that when people use

00:19:48.510 --> 00:19:54.210
Slides Explore, they create
their decks 60% faster than

00:19:54.210 --> 00:19:56.490
those that don't.

00:19:56.490 --> 00:19:57.976
So that's a lot of time.

00:19:57.976 --> 00:19:59.475
When you think about
building a deck

00:19:59.475 --> 00:20:02.820
and how much time
you spent on it--

00:20:02.820 --> 00:20:06.340
Slides Explore helped me
with this deck, by the way.

00:20:06.340 --> 00:20:12.330
But you think about how much
time you spend building a deck,

00:20:12.330 --> 00:20:16.020
and think about saving
60% of that time.

00:20:16.020 --> 00:20:19.410
Instead of focusing on how
you're going to tell the story,

00:20:19.410 --> 00:20:22.380
focus on the story you're
telling and the content you're

00:20:22.380 --> 00:20:23.850
putting in and
making sure that you

00:20:23.850 --> 00:20:27.060
have the information that you
need to get your point across.

00:20:27.060 --> 00:20:31.111
It, therefore, goes into
making meetings more effective.

00:20:31.111 --> 00:20:32.610
It goes into making
sure that you're

00:20:32.610 --> 00:20:34.560
telling the story you
should be telling,

00:20:34.560 --> 00:20:36.810
and lets you invest that
time into something that's

00:20:36.810 --> 00:20:38.910
more meaningful to
make sure that you're

00:20:38.910 --> 00:20:40.920
helping other people to
understand your point.

00:20:43.990 --> 00:20:47.530
Another kind of seemingly
simple example--

00:20:47.530 --> 00:20:50.440
within Calendar there's
features that we're

00:20:50.440 --> 00:20:54.820
doing, such as conflict
assistance, automated room

00:20:54.820 --> 00:20:59.620
booking and scheduling, finding
details about rooms so you

00:20:59.620 --> 00:21:02.740
can understand what
different functionality

00:21:02.740 --> 00:21:04.120
that room might have.

00:21:04.120 --> 00:21:07.554
Very simple things but things
that used to be kind of a pain

00:21:07.554 --> 00:21:09.970
point to figure out where you
needed to have your meeting,

00:21:09.970 --> 00:21:11.470
and whether there
were conflicts,

00:21:11.470 --> 00:21:14.925
and who needed to be there, and
what time they could be there.

00:21:14.925 --> 00:21:17.170
And so we're taking some
of those pain points

00:21:17.170 --> 00:21:19.870
and removing them and
making it much easier

00:21:19.870 --> 00:21:21.640
to schedule a meeting.

00:21:21.640 --> 00:21:24.460
And what we've measured is
that these small improvements

00:21:24.460 --> 00:21:27.310
save about three minutes
every time someone schedules

00:21:27.310 --> 00:21:28.420
a meeting.

00:21:28.420 --> 00:21:30.400
So you think about
how many meetings

00:21:30.400 --> 00:21:33.730
you schedule throughout the
week or throughout the month,

00:21:33.730 --> 00:21:36.814
and you take three minutes
for each time you did that,

00:21:36.814 --> 00:21:38.230
and you put it
back in your pocket

00:21:38.230 --> 00:21:40.660
to spend on something
more important.

00:21:40.660 --> 00:21:42.730
Something that has a
greater impact, something

00:21:42.730 --> 00:21:44.650
that helps you improve.

00:21:44.650 --> 00:21:47.680
So this, again, a
seemingly simple example

00:21:47.680 --> 00:21:51.170
that just goes to take more of
the pain out of doing your job

00:21:51.170 --> 00:21:56.560
and making sure you can
focus on having an impact.

00:21:56.560 --> 00:21:59.290
This example is
called Smart Reply.

00:21:59.290 --> 00:22:04.390
What Smart Reply does is
it generates simple replies

00:22:04.390 --> 00:22:07.660
to emails based on the
content of the email.

00:22:07.660 --> 00:22:10.630
So a simple thing--

00:22:10.630 --> 00:22:14.730
but what we've seen is that
within inbox on mobile,

00:22:14.730 --> 00:22:17.320
10% of all replies
are done using

00:22:17.320 --> 00:22:21.340
smart reply, which translates
into 15 minutes, per person,

00:22:21.340 --> 00:22:23.480
per day.

00:22:23.480 --> 00:22:27.610
Again, something where we're
taking time out of what you

00:22:27.610 --> 00:22:30.550
would normally be typing
on your mobile phone

00:22:30.550 --> 00:22:34.990
to try to respond to an email
and making it much more simple.

00:22:34.990 --> 00:22:40.120
Beyond just the time
savings, though, it also

00:22:40.120 --> 00:22:43.310
enables people to
respond more quickly.

00:22:43.310 --> 00:22:44.487
So when you read an email--

00:22:44.487 --> 00:22:46.570
I can't think of how many
times I've read an email

00:22:46.570 --> 00:22:48.940
and said I don't have
time to respond right now

00:22:48.940 --> 00:22:51.130
and that I'll come
back to it later.

00:22:51.130 --> 00:22:53.500
You think if you could
just respond very quickly

00:22:53.500 --> 00:22:56.200
you'd unblock the people that
are waiting for the information

00:22:56.200 --> 00:22:58.640
that they need to
continue forward.

00:22:58.640 --> 00:23:02.560
So beyond just the 15
minutes per person, per day,

00:23:02.560 --> 00:23:06.070
we're talking about unblocking
entire groups of people

00:23:06.070 --> 00:23:08.140
so they have the
information in context they

00:23:08.140 --> 00:23:10.960
need to move forward.

00:23:10.960 --> 00:23:13.120
And then this last
example that I

00:23:13.120 --> 00:23:14.950
want to talk about
in this section

00:23:14.950 --> 00:23:18.340
is actually a really big one.

00:23:18.340 --> 00:23:19.990
It's called Cloud Search.

00:23:19.990 --> 00:23:24.160
And MacKenzie did
a study that showed

00:23:24.160 --> 00:23:26.770
that knowledge workers
spend about 20%

00:23:26.770 --> 00:23:29.610
of their time looking
for information.

00:23:29.610 --> 00:23:31.480
20% of their time--

00:23:31.480 --> 00:23:34.480
that's the equivalent of
one out of every five people

00:23:34.480 --> 00:23:37.780
not showing up to
work in a given day.

00:23:37.780 --> 00:23:39.280
You think about the
impact that that

00:23:39.280 --> 00:23:41.590
has of people just
searching for things

00:23:41.590 --> 00:23:43.660
and not being able to find them.

00:23:43.660 --> 00:23:50.110
So Google-- we made our name
by organizing information

00:23:50.110 --> 00:23:52.610
and making it easy to find.

00:23:52.610 --> 00:23:55.030
We started by doing
that for the internet,

00:23:55.030 --> 00:23:58.030
and Cloud Search is
us starting to do

00:23:58.030 --> 00:23:59.682
that for your organization.

00:23:59.682 --> 00:24:01.390
We're taking all the
information that you

00:24:01.390 --> 00:24:04.270
have in different systems,
in different areas,

00:24:04.270 --> 00:24:07.900
and we're organizing it, and
we're making it easy for people

00:24:07.900 --> 00:24:09.550
to find.

00:24:09.550 --> 00:24:14.110
But beyond just search,
what we're also doing

00:24:14.110 --> 00:24:16.990
is suggestions
and notifications.

00:24:16.990 --> 00:24:20.740
So imagine that you're
preparing for a meeting,

00:24:20.740 --> 00:24:23.650
and you can automatically
be suggested

00:24:23.650 --> 00:24:26.800
meeting notes from
the previous time you

00:24:26.800 --> 00:24:28.540
met with the same group.

00:24:28.540 --> 00:24:30.550
You can be suggested
content that

00:24:30.550 --> 00:24:32.980
was attached to an
email or the meeting

00:24:32.980 --> 00:24:37.750
invite before the
meeting was held.

00:24:37.750 --> 00:24:41.950
You can be suggested
different content about things

00:24:41.950 --> 00:24:44.410
that you're working on that
are relevant to the topic

00:24:44.410 --> 00:24:45.677
of the meeting.

00:24:45.677 --> 00:24:48.010
So instead of having to go
and look for that information

00:24:48.010 --> 00:24:51.040
and to try to prepare
for your meeting,

00:24:51.040 --> 00:24:54.680
it can automatically be there,
waiting for you and ready.

00:24:54.680 --> 00:24:57.620
Which means you go, again, to
this meeting more prepared.

00:24:57.620 --> 00:24:59.620
The meeting is more
productive because everybody

00:24:59.620 --> 00:25:02.080
had the information
they need beforehand.

00:25:02.080 --> 00:25:03.700
And you make sure
that you accomplish

00:25:03.700 --> 00:25:06.850
the goal of why you're there.

00:25:06.850 --> 00:25:10.210
Another important part about the
suggestions and notifications

00:25:10.210 --> 00:25:12.070
is that a lot of
times people just

00:25:12.070 --> 00:25:15.250
don't think to search
for information.

00:25:15.250 --> 00:25:16.750
They don't think about--

00:25:16.750 --> 00:25:19.150
they don't know what they
don't know, you could say.

00:25:19.150 --> 00:25:21.160
So they might not be
thinking that they

00:25:21.160 --> 00:25:24.730
need to go look for
information about this topic,

00:25:24.730 --> 00:25:26.260
but we could
automatically suggest

00:25:26.260 --> 00:25:29.140
that it might be relevant based
on who you're meeting with

00:25:29.140 --> 00:25:31.120
or why you're going
to this meeting.

00:25:31.120 --> 00:25:33.155
So we're unlocking
more information,

00:25:33.155 --> 00:25:34.780
we're making it easy
to find, and we're

00:25:34.780 --> 00:25:40.620
making sure that you think
about it and it's there for you.

00:25:40.620 --> 00:25:43.530
So I've thrown a ton
of numbers at you.

00:25:43.530 --> 00:25:47.110
Almost every slide
had a number on it.

00:25:47.110 --> 00:25:49.080
So I just wanted to put
it all there for you

00:25:49.080 --> 00:25:50.580
so you could look at it.

00:25:50.580 --> 00:25:51.810
It's a great picture slide.

00:25:51.810 --> 00:25:55.140
I see a lot of people
taking advantage of that.

00:25:55.140 --> 00:25:57.930
But I don't want to go through
this line by line right now,

00:25:57.930 --> 00:26:00.930
I just want to touch
on a few of these.

00:26:00.930 --> 00:26:06.210
We talked about how we're taking
20% of a knowledge worker's

00:26:06.210 --> 00:26:09.330
time, and we're reducing
that to a fraction.

00:26:09.330 --> 00:26:12.750
So instead of searching
for 8 hours a week

00:26:12.750 --> 00:26:15.930
for the information they need,
they can simply search for it

00:26:15.930 --> 00:26:19.170
and find it, or it can be
suggested to them without them

00:26:19.170 --> 00:26:21.280
even thinking about it.

00:26:21.280 --> 00:26:23.790
We're taking 70% of
the organization that

00:26:23.790 --> 00:26:28.045
doesn't have access to the
data analysis they need,

00:26:28.045 --> 00:26:29.670
and we're making it
available for them.

00:26:29.670 --> 00:26:33.240
We're informing them and helping
them to make better decisions.

00:26:33.240 --> 00:26:36.300
We're saving 15 minutes
per person, per day,

00:26:36.300 --> 00:26:38.250
sending email,
making it very simple

00:26:38.250 --> 00:26:39.930
for them to send that off.

00:26:39.930 --> 00:26:44.160
And by enabling people to
work together on documents,

00:26:44.160 --> 00:26:47.460
we're saving 20% of their time
in creating those and helping

00:26:47.460 --> 00:26:50.040
them to be more prepared
and more in sync when

00:26:50.040 --> 00:26:51.420
they go to that meeting.

00:26:51.420 --> 00:26:54.990
So productivity
really is something

00:26:54.990 --> 00:26:59.574
that you should think about as
you think about total costs.

00:26:59.574 --> 00:27:01.740
All of these things are
things that you're currently

00:27:01.740 --> 00:27:04.120
spending money on in
your organization,

00:27:04.120 --> 00:27:05.740
and it could be more effective.

00:27:05.740 --> 00:27:07.170
That could be more productive.

00:27:07.170 --> 00:27:08.820
You could have that time back.

00:27:08.820 --> 00:27:10.200
And so we'd
encourage you, as you

00:27:10.200 --> 00:27:12.480
think about total costs--
and a lot of times

00:27:12.480 --> 00:27:15.180
when you think about cost,
you think about solid numbers

00:27:15.180 --> 00:27:18.547
that you're saving on
servers and infrastructure,

00:27:18.547 --> 00:27:20.880
but we'd encourage you to
also think about productivity.

00:27:20.880 --> 00:27:22.590
Because as Allan
talked about, it's

00:27:22.590 --> 00:27:29.160
not just about the ROI over
time of the light bulb.

00:27:29.160 --> 00:27:31.495
The CFL light bulb
did not take off

00:27:31.495 --> 00:27:33.120
because it wasn't
convenient, it didn't

00:27:33.120 --> 00:27:35.460
help improve the product.

00:27:35.460 --> 00:27:38.280
And so productivity
here is what we

00:27:38.280 --> 00:27:40.747
want you to think about first.

00:27:40.747 --> 00:27:42.330
And then I'm going
to pass it to Allan

00:27:42.330 --> 00:27:45.072
so he can talk a little
bit more about the costs

00:27:45.072 --> 00:27:47.280
and replacements that we're
offering through G Suite.

00:27:50.299 --> 00:27:51.590
ALLAN LIVINGSTON: Thanks, Chad.

00:27:51.590 --> 00:27:53.000
Yeah, and as Chad
said, we wanted

00:27:53.000 --> 00:27:54.650
to start with productivity.

00:27:54.650 --> 00:28:00.050
Because these numbers
here have the potential

00:28:00.050 --> 00:28:03.980
to be really transformative to
the way that companies operate.

00:28:03.980 --> 00:28:07.130
And you see that when
you see traditionally

00:28:07.130 --> 00:28:09.680
run companies to fully
cloud native companies,

00:28:09.680 --> 00:28:12.110
the real difference
in productivity.

00:28:12.110 --> 00:28:14.390
And it helps to explain
a lot of situations

00:28:14.390 --> 00:28:17.030
where people are working a lot
and not getting things done.

00:28:17.030 --> 00:28:18.770
It's because they're
spending their time

00:28:18.770 --> 00:28:21.530
doing these things that
are not productive.

00:28:21.530 --> 00:28:25.070
And the change, and
the move to the cloud,

00:28:25.070 --> 00:28:29.870
and the increasing prevalence
of useful machine learning,

00:28:29.870 --> 00:28:32.520
has the potential to
transform the way people work.

00:28:32.520 --> 00:28:35.360
So we think it's really the most
exciting and most compelling

00:28:35.360 --> 00:28:36.500
element on it.

00:28:36.500 --> 00:28:41.150
Now I'm going to switch and
talk about more traditional TCO

00:28:41.150 --> 00:28:44.630
elements, which is cost
reductions and savings.

00:28:44.630 --> 00:28:46.610
Because they're
substantial as well

00:28:46.610 --> 00:28:49.950
and have historically
been the primary element

00:28:49.950 --> 00:28:52.400
that people looked
at for the cloud.

00:28:56.260 --> 00:28:57.430
So we talked about--

00:28:57.430 --> 00:28:59.800
and here's a good quote
we've got from a customer.

00:28:59.800 --> 00:29:02.380
And so you've got 15%
increase in productivity

00:29:02.380 --> 00:29:04.530
and at 70% cheaper.

00:29:04.530 --> 00:29:05.290
OK?

00:29:05.290 --> 00:29:06.590
And where does that come from?

00:29:06.590 --> 00:29:10.810
Well, IT savings
is a great element.

00:29:10.810 --> 00:29:15.040
The average on-premise system
has very low utilization.

00:29:15.040 --> 00:29:19.376
It's really not safe
to run your servers

00:29:19.376 --> 00:29:20.500
at a very high utilization.

00:29:20.500 --> 00:29:22.300
You've got to keep a
lot of spare capacity,

00:29:22.300 --> 00:29:24.660
because you're going to
have really spiky demand.

00:29:24.660 --> 00:29:26.410
Everyone's coming
online at the same time,

00:29:26.410 --> 00:29:28.990
everyone's in at 9:00, no
one's there on Sundays.

00:29:28.990 --> 00:29:30.400
These type of things, right?

00:29:30.400 --> 00:29:31.900
Or you're going to
have other ones--

00:29:31.900 --> 00:29:34.040
you've got to have that
slack in the system.

00:29:34.040 --> 00:29:35.980
So it's really
expensive to maintain.

00:29:35.980 --> 00:29:38.200
It means that if
you're aggressive,

00:29:38.200 --> 00:29:43.030
82% of your capacity is IO
most of the time, maybe 88%.

00:29:43.030 --> 00:29:44.290
But it's an awful lot.

00:29:44.290 --> 00:29:48.730
And it's not just the cost of
the hardware-- it's the power,

00:29:48.730 --> 00:29:53.350
it's the space, the
maintenance as well.

00:29:53.350 --> 00:29:55.910
And the cloud architecture's
superior in that regard,

00:29:55.910 --> 00:30:00.460
because we amortized
it globally.

00:30:00.460 --> 00:30:02.440
And that takes a
spikiness out of it.

00:30:02.440 --> 00:30:06.550
And it really allows you to
more adequately and provision

00:30:06.550 --> 00:30:07.690
more correctly.

00:30:07.690 --> 00:30:10.810
So that you can provision the
amount of capacity you need,

00:30:10.810 --> 00:30:12.880
not the amount of
capacity you might

00:30:12.880 --> 00:30:15.170
need at one point in time.

00:30:18.090 --> 00:30:22.080
It also allows IT Com
staff to focus more

00:30:22.080 --> 00:30:23.170
on the core business.

00:30:23.170 --> 00:30:26.280
If you think about it,
running data centers

00:30:26.280 --> 00:30:30.920
is not the core business
of most companies.

00:30:30.920 --> 00:30:34.520
But if you're on-premise, that
needs to be a core capability.

00:30:34.520 --> 00:30:36.980
You've got to be good at
running data center scalably.

00:30:36.980 --> 00:30:40.130
And that focus takes
away, necessarily,

00:30:40.130 --> 00:30:42.120
from the focus on
the core business.

00:30:42.120 --> 00:30:44.120
We've heard some
examples of that

00:30:44.120 --> 00:30:45.980
this morning in the
Keynote, where companies

00:30:45.980 --> 00:30:47.480
were able to shed that.

00:30:47.480 --> 00:30:48.710
I like the Disney quote--

00:30:48.710 --> 00:30:49.880
they want to tell stories.

00:30:49.880 --> 00:30:51.740
They don't want to run
a bunch of servers,

00:30:51.740 --> 00:30:53.720
and they can focus
on that piece.

00:30:53.720 --> 00:30:56.240
And that freeing the IT staff
to focus on actual business

00:30:56.240 --> 00:31:00.675
problems is really valuable.

00:31:00.675 --> 00:31:03.260
Here's another example we
saw from City of Monterey--

00:31:03.260 --> 00:31:05.510
30% IT cost savings just
from these kind of elements

00:31:05.510 --> 00:31:08.224
that I talked about.

00:31:08.224 --> 00:31:09.390
Security is another big one.

00:31:12.602 --> 00:31:16.705
Google's got over 700 world
class security engineers.

00:31:20.320 --> 00:31:22.730
You can take those 700, and
they're working together

00:31:22.730 --> 00:31:25.020
to secure everything.

00:31:25.020 --> 00:31:28.910
But when you're on-premise,
you don't have 700 engineers.

00:31:28.910 --> 00:31:31.560
And it's sort of like what Eric
Schmidt talked about today.

00:31:31.560 --> 00:31:33.011
Is just not a good idea.

00:31:33.011 --> 00:31:34.010
Don't try to build that.

00:31:34.010 --> 00:31:36.800
Don't try to go hire
700 world class security

00:31:36.800 --> 00:31:41.420
engineers to work full time on
securing your on-premise data.

00:31:41.420 --> 00:31:43.160
But what it allows--

00:31:43.160 --> 00:31:46.720
by going to the cloud
companies can save all of that.

00:31:46.720 --> 00:31:50.060
They can also secure their data
more and be more effective.

00:31:52.850 --> 00:31:56.150
The idea is to remove
the physical boundaries

00:31:56.150 --> 00:31:57.740
that you have in your company.

00:31:57.740 --> 00:31:59.880
So traditionally, companies
were kind of locked in.

00:31:59.880 --> 00:32:03.020
If you're an on-premise,
you can access the data.

00:32:03.020 --> 00:32:05.520
That's not really
acceptable for nowadays,

00:32:05.520 --> 00:32:07.430
and so you've got
to get rid of that.

00:32:07.430 --> 00:32:09.764
But if you do that, then
you've got to secure everything

00:32:09.764 --> 00:32:11.221
because you've got
all these threat

00:32:11.221 --> 00:32:12.410
vectors can be coming in.

00:32:12.410 --> 00:32:15.200
So you can go and hire
700 security engineers,

00:32:15.200 --> 00:32:19.310
or you can deal with
occasional breaches.

00:32:19.310 --> 00:32:25.490
And data breaches are extremely
expensive to companies.

00:32:25.490 --> 00:32:27.800
For a global company,
the average data breach

00:32:27.800 --> 00:32:30.530
costs between $3.5
and $3.8 million.

00:32:30.530 --> 00:32:33.110
But obviously, many data
breaches that we see

00:32:33.110 --> 00:32:34.510
are actually more than that.

00:32:34.510 --> 00:32:38.570
They're an existential threat
to the company themselves--

00:32:38.570 --> 00:32:40.790
losing all of your
customer data,

00:32:40.790 --> 00:32:43.790
things like that, lack of
trust, the loss of trust.

00:32:43.790 --> 00:32:46.306
So this is a really
key element here.

00:32:46.306 --> 00:32:47.930
There's sort of the
superficial savings

00:32:47.930 --> 00:32:50.630
you see, and then the other
side, the downside risk

00:32:50.630 --> 00:32:54.380
that you've got if you don't
have adequate security.

00:32:54.380 --> 00:32:57.660
And agility is great.

00:32:57.660 --> 00:33:03.020
And so this is sort of the other
side of the utilization piece

00:33:03.020 --> 00:33:04.130
that I talked about.

00:33:04.130 --> 00:33:07.400
In an on-premise world,
you need to provision

00:33:07.400 --> 00:33:09.860
for your expected peak demand.

00:33:09.860 --> 00:33:13.680
So whatever peaky time you've
got from your workforce,

00:33:13.680 --> 00:33:16.490
you need to provision that
plus a little bit of a buffer.

00:33:16.490 --> 00:33:18.530
But you don't really
have any agility there.

00:33:18.530 --> 00:33:23.360
And the example
this morning of--

00:33:23.360 --> 00:33:27.260
I guess we lost our slides,
but I'll keep talking.

00:33:27.260 --> 00:33:30.830
So the example this
morning of Pokemon Go

00:33:30.830 --> 00:33:32.360
was a really good example.

00:33:32.360 --> 00:33:39.300
So the creators of that
experienced load that far, far

00:33:39.300 --> 00:33:41.280
exceeded what they expected.

00:33:41.280 --> 00:33:44.760
Immediately they exceeded
their lifetime projection.

00:33:44.760 --> 00:33:46.950
I can't even remember the
multiplier that it was.

00:33:46.950 --> 00:33:50.310
But if you think
about that, they

00:33:50.310 --> 00:33:52.365
wouldn't have been
able to deal with that

00:33:52.365 --> 00:33:56.010
in an on-premise model.

00:33:59.050 --> 00:34:02.620
So that agility allows you
to explore new businesses,

00:34:02.620 --> 00:34:07.090
and it allows you to also
focus on expanding quickly

00:34:07.090 --> 00:34:09.219
into new markets.

00:34:09.219 --> 00:34:12.409
And at this point, I'm actually
running out of content.

00:34:12.409 --> 00:34:14.949
So I'm hoping that we
can get the slides back.

00:34:14.949 --> 00:34:18.929
You have those cues where
you think of the next thing

00:34:18.929 --> 00:34:20.219
that you're going to do.

00:34:20.219 --> 00:34:22.989
I shall bravely soldier on here.

00:34:27.620 --> 00:34:30.554
Actually, I was going to talk
about the release process

00:34:30.554 --> 00:34:32.929
as well, because that came up
this morning on the agility

00:34:32.929 --> 00:34:34.710
side.

00:34:34.710 --> 00:34:37.340
And it was same thing,
the creator of Pokemon Go.

00:34:37.340 --> 00:34:39.980
If you think about
how that works,

00:34:39.980 --> 00:34:44.330
they were able to not
focus on building out

00:34:44.330 --> 00:34:48.362
this IT infrastructure-- as was
Snap, as well, for Snapchat.

00:34:48.362 --> 00:34:49.820
And what that
allowed them to do is

00:34:49.820 --> 00:34:51.861
it allowed them to have
a much faster development

00:34:51.861 --> 00:34:55.221
process because they were freed
from all of that other load.

00:34:55.221 --> 00:34:56.929
So that agility brings
you these benefits

00:34:56.929 --> 00:35:00.320
of being able to release
software and develop

00:35:00.320 --> 00:35:03.690
faster and solve customer
problems a lot faster.

00:35:03.690 --> 00:35:05.349
And I think we're almost there.

00:35:05.349 --> 00:35:06.140
We're almost there?

00:35:06.140 --> 00:35:07.199
Good.

00:35:07.199 --> 00:35:08.990
And another one that
I wanted to talk about

00:35:08.990 --> 00:35:10.550
was change management.

00:35:10.550 --> 00:35:14.990
Change management is
a cost, obviously.

00:35:14.990 --> 00:35:18.200
Anytime your employees are
going through a change,

00:35:18.200 --> 00:35:19.890
you've got to
manage that change.

00:35:19.890 --> 00:35:21.431
You don't have to
manage that change,

00:35:21.431 --> 00:35:23.720
you can let the employees
deal with it themselves,

00:35:23.720 --> 00:35:25.810
but oftentimes that
is not advisable.

00:35:25.810 --> 00:35:28.424
And so one of the
concerns people

00:35:28.424 --> 00:35:29.840
have sometimes
when they're moving

00:35:29.840 --> 00:35:32.756
the cloud is they think
about change management.

00:35:32.756 --> 00:35:34.130
How expensive
that's going to be,

00:35:34.130 --> 00:35:36.230
that it's going to
be a big undertaking.

00:35:36.230 --> 00:35:38.360
And I think you heard
some examples this morning

00:35:38.360 --> 00:35:39.080
at the Keynote.

00:35:39.080 --> 00:35:42.860
Where you've got some customers
coming in and activating

00:35:42.860 --> 00:35:46.070
20,000 employees over the
weekend or things like that.

00:35:46.070 --> 00:35:47.990
And what we've found
with customers moving

00:35:47.990 --> 00:35:51.560
to the cloud is because the
experiences are kind of what

00:35:51.560 --> 00:35:53.420
they're used to and
what they expect,

00:35:53.420 --> 00:35:58.094
the change management process is
really, really easy and simple.

00:35:58.094 --> 00:35:59.510
And in fact, the
average payback--

00:35:59.510 --> 00:36:01.760
when we looked at
that Forrester study

00:36:01.760 --> 00:36:04.010
that Chad talked about--
the average payback

00:36:04.010 --> 00:36:07.020
for a change management
program was less than a month.

00:36:07.020 --> 00:36:09.320
So even more ridiculous
than the payback

00:36:09.320 --> 00:36:13.250
that we talked about
for the LED light bulbs.

00:36:13.250 --> 00:36:17.156
That effectively
it's instant payback.

00:36:17.156 --> 00:36:19.280
There's very lightweight
change management process,

00:36:19.280 --> 00:36:20.780
you roll people out
over the weekend,

00:36:20.780 --> 00:36:22.404
you go, and then it's
really effective.

00:36:22.404 --> 00:36:25.430
And so that's something
that we think is-- very much

00:36:25.430 --> 00:36:28.190
plays into.

00:36:28.190 --> 00:36:29.140
Here we go.

