00:37:39.450 --> 00:37:43.920
Data loss prevention allows
you to set customized policies,

00:37:43.920 --> 00:37:49.100
so that depending on the content
that you're seeing in documents

00:37:49.100 --> 00:37:51.889
or e-mails, you're controlling
the sharing settings,

00:37:51.889 --> 00:37:53.430
or who can send what
to where, or how

00:37:53.430 --> 00:37:55.230
you trigger a report on that.

00:37:55.230 --> 00:37:58.380
And the cost of data
loss prevention software

00:37:58.380 --> 00:38:03.210
varies, but sort of a
typical market example

00:38:03.210 --> 00:38:06.130
would be around $2
a month per user.

00:38:06.130 --> 00:38:08.300
And that's just
included in the G Suite.

00:38:14.030 --> 00:38:14.940
No.

00:38:14.940 --> 00:38:17.210
We're not-- the
clicker, no, we're not.

00:38:17.210 --> 00:38:20.870
So we're getting there.

00:38:20.870 --> 00:38:23.840
It doesn't click through.

00:38:23.840 --> 00:38:24.500
OK.

00:38:24.500 --> 00:38:28.340
So the next one-- let's
talk about e-discovery.

00:38:28.340 --> 00:38:30.812
So in order to
comply-- companies

00:38:30.812 --> 00:38:33.020
have to comply with legal
requests and data discovery

00:38:33.020 --> 00:38:35.561
elements, and so they need an
e-discovery in our cloud native

00:38:35.561 --> 00:38:36.440
system.

00:38:36.440 --> 00:38:39.770
And the G Suite includes
that with Vault.

00:38:39.770 --> 00:38:42.050
And we looked at
the market, and we

00:38:42.050 --> 00:38:45.770
found that systems like
that can cause-- the cost

00:38:45.770 --> 00:38:47.630
is variable again,
but a lot of times

00:38:47.630 --> 00:38:52.800
a market reference price would
be about $60 a year per user.

00:38:52.800 --> 00:38:54.780
Which again is pretty material.

00:38:54.780 --> 00:38:55.970
Let's see if we can do that.

00:38:55.970 --> 00:38:56.180
OK.

00:38:56.180 --> 00:38:56.630
Here we go.

00:38:56.630 --> 00:38:57.170
Perfect.

00:38:57.170 --> 00:38:58.500
And so there's e-discovery.

00:38:58.500 --> 00:38:59.890
Let's keep going on that.

00:38:59.890 --> 00:39:02.780
Another one's
identity as a service.

00:39:02.780 --> 00:39:04.580
And so there's
this transformation

00:39:04.580 --> 00:39:07.580
that's happening in the cloud,
in IT infrastructure right now.

00:39:07.580 --> 00:39:11.480
Obviously, everyone's moving
from an on-premise way of doing

00:39:11.480 --> 00:39:13.520
your directory into the cloud.

00:39:13.520 --> 00:39:17.060
And you can either buy a
point solution for that,

00:39:17.060 --> 00:39:19.355
or you can have one
integrated with the G Suite.

00:39:19.355 --> 00:39:21.396
And the price of this is
actually quite variable.

00:39:21.396 --> 00:39:23.090
You're going to
see prices anywhere

00:39:23.090 --> 00:39:26.210
from $12 a year per user,
up to $72 a year per user,

00:39:26.210 --> 00:39:27.866
depending on the functionality.

00:39:27.866 --> 00:39:29.240
And we're continuing
to build out

00:39:29.240 --> 00:39:30.560
these capabilities in G Suite.

00:39:30.560 --> 00:39:31.670
So the advantages are--

00:39:31.670 --> 00:39:32.660
you go with the G
Suite, you can get

00:39:32.660 --> 00:39:34.370
rid of all of these
category items.

00:39:34.370 --> 00:39:37.070
You just cross them right out.

00:39:37.070 --> 00:39:39.710
One more-- mobility management.

00:39:39.710 --> 00:39:41.540
How are you securing
your devices?

00:39:41.540 --> 00:39:43.109
I said we're in
multi-device world,

00:39:43.109 --> 00:39:44.150
you need to secure those.

00:39:44.150 --> 00:39:46.020
You need to set policies
on your devices.

00:39:46.020 --> 00:39:47.900
You need to control what
kind of applications

00:39:47.900 --> 00:39:50.570
are installed on your
devices by your users.

00:39:50.570 --> 00:39:53.150
You need to occasionally remote
wipe them if they're lost.

00:39:53.150 --> 00:39:56.777
All that is provided by
Enterprise Mobility Management.

00:39:56.777 --> 00:39:59.360
Some people would traditionally
have called this Mobile Device

00:39:59.360 --> 00:40:01.210
Management.

00:40:01.210 --> 00:40:03.210
Average market price
you're seeing is around $54

00:40:03.210 --> 00:40:05.800
a user per year.

00:40:05.800 --> 00:40:08.812
So all that adds
up to an awful lot.

00:40:08.812 --> 00:40:10.520
We talked about the
productivity savings.

00:40:10.520 --> 00:40:12.470
We talked about the
IT cost savings.

00:40:12.470 --> 00:40:15.050
And we also talked about the
replacement cost savings.

00:40:15.050 --> 00:40:19.810
So Chad's going to run you
through the total cost,

00:40:19.810 --> 00:40:22.990
TCO calculator, in a
little bit more detail

00:40:22.990 --> 00:40:24.310
and how that all fits together.

00:40:24.310 --> 00:40:26.874
Thank you.

00:40:26.874 --> 00:40:28.082
CHAD TYLER: Thank you, Allan.

00:40:33.038 --> 00:40:33.538
Sorry.

00:40:39.000 --> 00:40:39.600
OK.

00:40:39.600 --> 00:40:40.637
There we go.

00:40:40.637 --> 00:40:41.220
Thanks, Allan.

00:40:41.220 --> 00:40:43.650
So we've talked
about a lot of costs

00:40:43.650 --> 00:40:46.770
and a lot of things
that feed into costs.

00:40:46.770 --> 00:40:49.080
However, total
cost of ownership,

00:40:49.080 --> 00:40:53.610
it varies largely
between companies

00:40:53.610 --> 00:40:55.780
based on where
you're coming from,

00:40:55.780 --> 00:40:58.930
or what you're trying to do,
and where you want to end up.

00:40:58.930 --> 00:41:02.490
So it's hard sometimes
to understand

00:41:02.490 --> 00:41:07.710
exactly what TCO will look like
in your specific organization.

00:41:07.710 --> 00:41:11.790
And that's why we built a total
cost of ownership calculator.

00:41:11.790 --> 00:41:14.550
What this allows
you to do is enter

00:41:14.550 --> 00:41:17.460
in your company
information, details

00:41:17.460 --> 00:41:20.040
about where you operate,
what you're trying to do,

00:41:20.040 --> 00:41:23.615
how many people you have
within your organization.

00:41:23.615 --> 00:41:28.500
It lets you enter in what
you're using right now as

00:41:28.500 --> 00:41:31.740
far as software hardware
and productivity,

00:41:31.740 --> 00:41:33.600
so that you can enter
in things that are

00:41:33.600 --> 00:41:35.940
very specific to your company.

00:41:35.940 --> 00:41:38.130
And then what it
does is it generates

00:41:38.130 --> 00:41:42.420
a picture of what you
might be able to expect

00:41:42.420 --> 00:41:45.390
switching to G Suite.

00:41:45.390 --> 00:41:47.430
So it breaks things
down based on where

00:41:47.430 --> 00:41:52.500
you might see savings, what
your return on investment

00:41:52.500 --> 00:41:55.080
might be, and kind of gives
you a better picture, very

00:41:55.080 --> 00:41:58.790
specific to you, what
you might expect.

00:41:58.790 --> 00:42:01.920
And so it's a little bit
more applicable directly

00:42:01.920 --> 00:42:06.220
to what you're coming from
and what you're looking at.

00:42:06.220 --> 00:42:09.080
So this is available
for you to use.

00:42:09.080 --> 00:42:11.950
The easiest way to find it is
probably just to go to Google

00:42:11.950 --> 00:42:15.970
and search for G
Suite TCO Calculator.

00:42:15.970 --> 00:42:17.860
The first two
results that come up

00:42:17.860 --> 00:42:20.080
will be two different
versions of a calculator

00:42:20.080 --> 00:42:21.250
that you can use.

00:42:21.250 --> 00:42:24.520
As you can see by the
purple, I've clicked on both.

00:42:24.520 --> 00:42:30.442
But the first version, it
relates to the Forrester study

00:42:30.442 --> 00:42:31.900
that we've referenced
several times

00:42:31.900 --> 00:42:34.540
throughout this presentation,
based on some of the results

00:42:34.540 --> 00:42:37.960
they've found, and
more to productivity

00:42:37.960 --> 00:42:39.800
and different
elements like that.

00:42:39.800 --> 00:42:42.430
And the second one is
more general purpose,

00:42:42.430 --> 00:42:47.830
where you can see what you might
expect coming from on-premise,

00:42:47.830 --> 00:42:50.740
or hybrid, or a
different cloud provider,

00:42:50.740 --> 00:42:56.140
and just kind of gives you
a better picture of the cost

00:42:56.140 --> 00:42:58.270
that you could
save and the impact

00:42:58.270 --> 00:43:01.900
that G Suite could have on
your total cost of ownership.

00:43:01.900 --> 00:43:05.276
So we'd encourage you to
go and check those out just

00:43:05.276 --> 00:43:07.150
to get a better picture
of some of the things

00:43:07.150 --> 00:43:09.670
we've talked about today
more directly applicable

00:43:09.670 --> 00:43:11.544
to your specific company.

00:43:11.544 --> 00:43:12.960
So I want to pass
it back to Allan

00:43:12.960 --> 00:43:15.860
so he can summarize some of
the things we've talked about.

00:43:15.860 --> 00:43:16.860
ALLAN LIVINGSTON: Great.

00:43:16.860 --> 00:43:17.760
Thanks, Chad.

00:43:17.760 --> 00:43:21.340
And so just to recap
where we're at.

00:43:21.340 --> 00:43:24.670
We have these three areas that
we wanted to focus on TCO--

00:43:24.670 --> 00:43:28.490
productivity, cost reduction,
replacement savings.

00:43:28.490 --> 00:43:31.410
And we created a convenient
cheat sheet here at the end,

00:43:31.410 --> 00:43:32.830
so let's run through these.

00:43:32.830 --> 00:43:36.040
And the productivity
benefits we talked about

00:43:36.040 --> 00:43:37.870
and Chad had talked
about in detail--

00:43:37.870 --> 00:43:45.070
and in my mind, these feel like
the most compelling elements

00:43:45.070 --> 00:43:47.710
of any cost strategy.

00:43:47.710 --> 00:43:49.920
You're talking about
helping employees--

00:43:49.920 --> 00:43:50.920
and these all add up.

00:43:50.920 --> 00:43:56.050
So reducing the amount of this
20% that information workers

00:43:56.050 --> 00:43:58.780
spend finding the correct
information, or in some cases,

00:43:58.780 --> 00:44:01.990
not finding it, because that
can be even more expensive.

00:44:01.990 --> 00:44:05.620
Helping people spend
less time sending email.

00:44:05.620 --> 00:44:08.020
And Chad only talked
about one element of that,

00:44:08.020 --> 00:44:10.750
we're doing a whole bunch more
on inbox to even bring that

00:44:10.750 --> 00:44:12.350
down further.

00:44:12.350 --> 00:44:14.890
Helping you spend less
time scheduling a meeting.

00:44:14.890 --> 00:44:17.380
Average information
worker spends up

00:44:17.380 --> 00:44:20.590
to 300 minutes a month
scheduling meetings.

00:44:20.590 --> 00:44:24.070
So bringing that down and
all the frustration that that

00:44:24.070 --> 00:44:25.000
brings with it.

00:44:25.000 --> 00:44:27.606
And the suboptimal
scheduling-- right?

00:44:27.606 --> 00:44:29.980
The computers are much better
at these kind of scheduling

00:44:29.980 --> 00:44:31.600
problems than humans.

00:44:31.600 --> 00:44:35.230
And so the productivity
gains are there.

00:44:35.230 --> 00:44:38.470
Being able to complete
slides fundamentally faster.

00:44:38.470 --> 00:44:40.450
And this one is
really interesting.

00:44:40.450 --> 00:44:42.850
Because I remember when
I first saw this feature,

00:44:42.850 --> 00:44:45.130
I was a little skeptical of it.

00:44:45.130 --> 00:44:47.590
What kind of did it for
me was-- so I was at home,

00:44:47.590 --> 00:44:51.820
and I have two elementary
school children, and one of them

00:44:51.820 --> 00:44:54.340
was doing a report.

00:44:54.340 --> 00:44:57.710
And he was sitting there
getting the slides lined up

00:44:57.710 --> 00:44:59.470
and getting all the
images lined up.

00:44:59.470 --> 00:45:02.740
And the other one walked
by and looked at him

00:45:02.740 --> 00:45:06.490
and said, just use Slides
Explore already, and rolled

00:45:06.490 --> 00:45:08.770
his eyes and walked away.

00:45:08.770 --> 00:45:10.610
And it told me two things--

00:45:10.610 --> 00:45:14.302
one, I've got to do a better
job in parenting overall.

00:45:14.302 --> 00:45:16.010
Kind of got to watch
that attitude a bit.

00:45:16.010 --> 00:45:18.040
But then the other
point was just

00:45:18.040 --> 00:45:19.980
how easy this stuff was
to use even for kids.

00:45:19.980 --> 00:45:23.740
And that it was so obvious
to them the value of it.

00:45:23.740 --> 00:45:26.260
And so that's a
huge element there.

00:45:26.260 --> 00:45:28.840
40% reduction in travel costs--

00:45:28.840 --> 00:45:31.600
I, myself, actually travel
quite a lot less now

00:45:31.600 --> 00:45:32.690
because of video meetings.

00:45:32.690 --> 00:45:35.057
I've found that the
way that you work now

00:45:35.057 --> 00:45:37.390
versus the way you'd work
five years ago or 10 years ago

00:45:37.390 --> 00:45:38.080
changes.

00:45:38.080 --> 00:45:39.870
And that's really,
really effective.

00:45:39.870 --> 00:45:42.250
More employee satisfaction,
more for retaining,

00:45:42.250 --> 00:45:43.552
people analyzing data.

00:45:43.552 --> 00:45:45.260
So you've got more
people analyzing data,

00:45:45.260 --> 00:45:47.426
and the people that already
knew how to analyze data

00:45:47.426 --> 00:45:50.050
can do it much, much faster
and can do a better job.

00:45:50.050 --> 00:45:51.430
Less time creating documents.

00:45:51.430 --> 00:45:53.320
We didn't even
talk about the fact

00:45:53.320 --> 00:45:55.820
that the documents are better
because you're doing real time

00:45:55.820 --> 00:45:56.640
co-editing on them.

00:45:56.640 --> 00:45:58.120
So they're actually
higher quality,

00:45:58.120 --> 00:46:00.530
and they're more reflective
of the team's output.

00:46:00.530 --> 00:46:04.790
So huge benefits here on
the productivity side.

00:46:04.790 --> 00:46:08.690
Cost reductions-- the
utilization factor

00:46:08.690 --> 00:46:11.000
that is just inherent.

00:46:11.000 --> 00:46:14.140
There's an inherent difference
in a centralized cloud model

00:46:14.140 --> 00:46:15.460
and a siloed on-premise model.

00:46:15.460 --> 00:46:17.168
There's really no way
to get around that.

00:46:17.168 --> 00:46:20.660
That's one of the first
reasons that cloud caught on.

00:46:20.660 --> 00:46:23.030
And it's obvious, you just
can't change the math.

00:46:23.030 --> 00:46:25.970
Taking a bunch of spiky loads
and averaging them together

00:46:25.970 --> 00:46:27.440
you get a flatter
load that allows

00:46:27.440 --> 00:46:31.600
you to have better
utilization of resources.

00:46:31.600 --> 00:46:34.020
Being able to specialize--

00:46:34.020 --> 00:46:37.140
it's kind of strange
that we've ever

00:46:37.140 --> 00:46:39.990
got to this model where it was
expected that every company had

00:46:39.990 --> 00:46:43.090
to become an expert in
running a data center.

00:46:43.090 --> 00:46:44.590
That seems like an
odd thing, right?

00:46:44.590 --> 00:46:46.962
Like every company is an
expert in every other field,

00:46:46.962 --> 00:46:48.420
why would they have
to be an expert

00:46:48.420 --> 00:46:49.461
in running a data center?

00:46:49.461 --> 00:46:51.930
It's a very
specific, niche task.

00:46:51.930 --> 00:46:53.910
And there's not
really much reason

00:46:53.910 --> 00:46:55.920
for a manufacturing
company to be an expert

00:46:55.920 --> 00:46:57.700
in running a data center.

00:46:57.700 --> 00:46:59.520
And so you can specialize
and your IT staff

00:46:59.520 --> 00:47:01.560
can focus on solving
problems that

00:47:01.560 --> 00:47:03.780
are relevant to your industry.

00:47:03.780 --> 00:47:06.960
Security-- we talked about
the threat and the importance

00:47:06.960 --> 00:47:09.110
of security is going
up all the time.

00:47:09.110 --> 00:47:11.460
You can look at all
of the recent data

00:47:11.460 --> 00:47:12.750
breaches that are in the news.

00:47:12.750 --> 00:47:14.920
Everyone knows that's just
the tip of the iceberg.

00:47:14.920 --> 00:47:16.170
It is happening more and more.

00:47:16.170 --> 00:47:18.384
And this is an existential
threat to companies.

00:47:18.384 --> 00:47:20.550
And so they've got to make
sure you're staying ahead

00:47:20.550 --> 00:47:23.280
of these ever increasing
and ever more sophisticated

00:47:23.280 --> 00:47:25.060
security threats.

00:47:25.060 --> 00:47:27.606
And then, of course, agility--

00:47:27.606 --> 00:47:29.730
the one thing, again, from
the keynote this morning

00:47:29.730 --> 00:47:32.610
that really struck me was the
stat that Diane gave about how

00:47:32.610 --> 00:47:36.240
the average age of companies
now is significantly

00:47:36.240 --> 00:47:40.630
less than the average age
of companies 20 years ago,

00:47:40.630 --> 00:47:41.880
40 years ago.

00:47:41.880 --> 00:47:42.750
And you see that.

00:47:42.750 --> 00:47:45.690
You see whole industries
being turned over

00:47:45.690 --> 00:47:47.190
and the players changing.

00:47:47.190 --> 00:47:49.120
And so you've got to be agile.

00:47:49.120 --> 00:47:51.420
You've got to be able to
adapt to those changes.

00:47:51.420 --> 00:47:53.310
Again, you can see
them as cost savings,

00:47:53.310 --> 00:47:55.650
but they're also just
fundamental to being

00:47:55.650 --> 00:47:57.275
successful in business.

00:47:57.275 --> 00:47:59.900
And then the last piece, this is
a little more straightforward,

00:47:59.900 --> 00:48:00.680
the math.

00:48:00.680 --> 00:48:02.370
Just flat out replacement.

00:48:02.370 --> 00:48:04.200
Replacing point
solutions with a suite.

00:48:04.200 --> 00:48:05.070
It's more efficient.

00:48:05.070 --> 00:48:08.100
It seems to be the way
that the industry is going,

00:48:08.100 --> 00:48:11.430
and we're firm
believers in that.

00:48:11.430 --> 00:48:15.900
We obviously support
all of these categories

00:48:15.900 --> 00:48:19.061
inherent in the product, and we
partner with the best in class

00:48:19.061 --> 00:48:19.810
people everywhere.

00:48:19.810 --> 00:48:21.900
But for a lot of people,
an integrated package

00:48:21.900 --> 00:48:24.420
is going to work really well.

00:48:24.420 --> 00:48:28.620
And so with that I
wanted to wrap up and say

00:48:28.620 --> 00:48:30.210
thanks, everyone, for coming.

00:48:30.210 --> 00:48:32.960
